pureldap Plugin for DokuWiki

Universal LDAP authentication for DokuWiki. Replaces the bundled authad
and authldap plugins with a single backend built on the FreeDSx pure-PHP
LDAP library.

All documentation for this plugin can be found at
https://www.dokuwiki.org/plugin:pureldap

If you install this plugin manually, make sure it is installed in
lib/plugins/pureldap/ - if the folder is called different it
will not work!

Please refer to http://www.dokuwiki.org/plugins for additional info
on how to install plugins in DokuWiki.

----
Picking a directory type

pureldap supports two modes, selected with the `directory_type` config
option (default `ad` for backwards compatibility):

  * `ad`   - Active Directory. Pre-seeds AD's schema as defaults
             (sAMAccountName + userPrincipalName, displayName,
             objectClass=user/group, memberOf, primaryGroupID, ...).
             Decodes pwdlastset / userAccountControl for password
             expiry, knows the AD bind error sub-codes, applies the UPN
             suffix during bind, and handles the unicodePwd UTF-16LE
             password change protocol.

  * `ldap` - Any RFC 4511 LDAP server: OpenLDAP, FreeIPA, 389DS, etc.
             All schema details are configurable; sensible posix
             defaults are pre-seeded.

----
Migrating from authad

  authad option       -> pureldap option
  -------------       -------------------
  account_suffix      -> suffix
  base_dn             -> base_dn
  domain_controllers  -> servers (comma-list)
  use_ssl             -> encryption = ssl
  use_tls             -> encryption = tls
  admin_username      -> admin_username
  admin_password      -> admin_password
  real_primarygroup   -> (implicit; primaryGroupID=513 is always honoured)
  additional          -> attributes
  expirywarn          -> expirywarn
  recursive_groups    -> recursivegroups
  update_pass         -> implicit via encryption setting
  sso                 -> sso
  sso_charset         -> sso_charset

  Set directory_type = ad (the default).

Known regressions for authad users:
  * Multi-domain dispatch (per-domain config sub-arrays + UI domain
    selector) is not yet implemented. Single-domain forests work fine.
  * update_name / update_mail are not implemented (pureldap supports
    password changes only, same as authldap).

----
Migrating from authldap

  authldap option  -> pureldap option
  ---------------  -------------------
  server           -> servers (comma-list)
  port             -> port
  usertree         -> usertree
  grouptree        -> grouptree
  userfilter       -> userfilter
  groupfilter      -> groupfilter
  userscope        -> userscope
  groupscope       -> groupscope
  userkey          -> userkey
  groupkey         -> groupkey
  starttls         -> encryption = tls
  binddn + bindpw  -> binddn (template) or admin_username + admin_password
  attributes       -> attributes
  modPass          -> modPass
  modPassPlain     -> modPassPlain

  Set directory_type = ldap.

Bind mode selection:
  * binddn template with %{user}: direct user bind (no admin lookup).
  * admin_username + admin_password set: service-account bind, then
    search-then-bind as the user. This is the closest equivalent to
    authldap's "anonymous or service-account search + user bind" flow.

Group resolution (group_strategy):
  * `auto` (default): grouptree if groupfilter is set, else memberOf if
    the user entry carries it, else just defaultgroup.
  * `grouptree`: RFC 2307 / posixGroup pattern with groupfilter.
  * `memberof`: walk the memberOf attribute (requires the memberOf
    overlay on OpenLDAP / FreeIPA / 389DS). With recursivegroups=1 the
    nested-group hierarchy is followed via GroupHierarchyCache.
  * `none`: just defaultgroup.

Known regressions for authldap users:
  * The `version`, `referrals`, and `deref` options are not exposed.
    FreeDSx speaks LDAPv3 only; referral and dereferencing behaviour
    follow FreeDSx defaults.
  * The advanced `mapping` array (attribute + regex extraction) is not
    yet ported. `namekey` and `userkey` accept comma-separated fallback
    lists which covers the most common case.

